# Executive brief: profit-aware retention targeting

**To:** Head of Customer Retention | **From:** Retention analytics team | **Decision requested:** approve a 90-day retention pilot

## Recommendation

Stop allocating the retention budget to "the customers most likely to leave". Allocate it to the customers for whom **churn risk x customer value x the offer's save rate** most exceeds the cost of the offer, and give each customer an offer their segment qualifies for. Start with a **randomised pilot** on the recommended list: one wave of {{ pilot.list_size_per_wave | num }} customers ({{ pilot.contacted_share | pct(0) }} of the {{ pilot.base_population | num }}-customer base), half held out as a control group. Scale up only if the measured save rate clears that list's **{{ rbe | pct }} break-even**.

## Why: the same budget returns more

Results for {{ budget.n_test | num }} held-out customers with a fixed {{ budget.base | usd }} budget (enough to offer {{ budget.share | pct(0) }} of them the standard offer):

| Option | Customers contacted | Expected net value | ROI | Precision among contacted | High-risk/high-value coverage |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| Do nothing | 0 | $0 | n/a | n/a | 0.0% |
| Call highest churn risk | {{ c.risk.selected_count | num }} | {{ c.risk.expected_net_value | usd }} | {{ c.risk.roi | pct(0) }} | {{ c.risk.precision_contacted | pct }} | {{ c.risk.high_risk_high_value_coverage | pct }} |
| Expected value, standard offer | {{ c.ev.selected_count | num }} | {{ c.ev.expected_net_value | usd }} | {{ c.ev.roi | pct(0) }} | {{ c.ev.precision_contacted | pct }} | {{ c.ev.high_risk_high_value_coverage | pct }} |
| **Expected value + segment offers** | **{{ c.segment_offers.selected_count | num }}** | **{{ c.segment_offers.expected_net_value | usd }}** | **{{ c.segment_offers.roi | pct(0) }}** | **{{ c.segment_offers.precision_contacted | pct }}** | **{{ c.segment_offers.high_risk_high_value_coverage | pct }}** |

- **Better targeting earns {{ headline.targeting_effect | usd }} more ({{ (headline.targeting_effect / c.risk.expected_net_value) | pct(0) }}) for the same spend.** Using what these customers actually did, the gain is {{ unc['ev minus risk'].label_based_simulated_net | usd }} (95% interval {{ unc['ev minus risk'].lower_95 | usd }} to {{ unc['ev minus risk'].upper_95 | usd }}).
- **Why:** risk-only targeting spends the budget on high-risk customers whose remaining value is too small to repay an offer. It reaches only {{ c.risk.high_risk_high_value_coverage | pct(0) }} of the high-risk, high-value customers, against {{ chosen.high_risk_high_value_coverage | pct(0) }} for the recommended rule. It has higher precision but less value.
- **Segment offers add {{ headline.offer_design_effect | usd }}** under the assumed save rates. Checked against actual outcomes the gain is not distinguishable from zero ({{ unc['segment_offers minus ev'].label_based_simulated_net | usd }}), so the pilot must test the offers, not just the list.

## How much to spend

Each extra budget step returns less (figure). We keep only the steps that still earn at least a {{ recommendation.hurdle_marginal_roi | pct(0) }} marginal return; this was decided on validation data. That rule recommends **{{ budget.recommended_per_base_customer | usd(2) }} per customer in the base** (enough for the standard offer to {{ recommendation.recommended_share | pct(0) }} of customers; about {{ scaled.recommended_budget | usd }} for all {{ scaled.base_population | num }}). On the test customers this buys {{ recommended.selected_count | num }} offers ({{ recommended.contacted_share | pct(0) }} of customers) for {{ recommended.cost | usd }}, an expected net value of {{ recommended.expected_net_value | usd }} (ROI {{ recommended.roi | pct(0) }}). Beyond {{ curve.positive_ev_share | pct(0) }} of customers contacted, nobody left has positive expected value, so further budget should not be spent.

