## How sure are we?

- **The model ranks customers well:** ROC-AUC {{ model.selected_test.roc_auc | num(2) }}, PR-AUC {{ model.selected_test.average_precision | num(2) }} (random contact: {{ data.churn_rate | num(2) }}). Predicted churn is within {{ (model.selected_test.ece * 100) | num(1) }} percentage points of observed churn on average. It predicts who leaves, not who an offer would keep.
- **Break-even:** the base-budget list stays profitable while the true save rate is above **{{ be | pct }}**, the larger recommended list above **{{ rbe | pct }}**; we assume {{ default_offer.save_rate | pct(0) }}. Below {{ break_even.minimum_viable_save_rate | pct }} nobody is worth contacting.
- **Scenarios:** expected net value is {{ scenarios.pessimistic.expected_net_value | usd }} pessimistic (no campaign is run), {{ scenarios.base.expected_net_value | usd }} base and {{ scenarios.optimistic.expected_net_value | usd }} optimistic. A list frozen under the pessimistic case would lose {{ (-scenarios.pessimistic.fixed_expected_net_value) | usd }}.
- **Biggest uncertainties:** {{ tornado[0].label | lower }} and {{ tornado[1].label | lower }}.

## Risks and guardrails

- **No causal evidence yet.** Save rates are documented assumptions. Contacting customers can even raise churn, so the pilot keeps a random control group.
- **Sample data.** The IBM sample describes a fictional US company with {{ data.churn_rate | pct }} churn; real US carriers lose about 1% of postpaid customers per month. The results show that the method works, not how big the prize is. Retrain on our own data before rollout.
- **Fairness and experience.** Senior customers are contacted {{ fairness_by[strategy ~ '|SeniorCitizen|1'].ratio_to_reference | ratio(1) }} as often as others, because they are higher-risk and pay more; age and gender are not model inputs. Loyal, low-risk customers rarely get offers, so watch for "loyalty penalty" complaints.
- **Compliance (Vietnam).** Prior consent and a Do-Not-Call check are required. Advertising calls are limited to one per 24 hours, between 08:00 and 17:00 (Decree 91/2020). A data-protection impact assessment is required under the Personal Data Protection Law 2025.

## Decisions requested

1. Approve one pilot wave: {{ pilot.arm_size_per_wave | num }} customers contacted and {{ pilot.arm_size_per_wave | num }} held out. Offer and contact budget about {{ pilot.wave_cost | usd }}.
2. Name owners: Retention (offers and script), Call centre (capacity), Finance (margin and offer cost), Legal/DPO (consent and DPIA).
3. Agree the decision rule now. After {{ pilot.outcome_window_days }} days: **go** (scale to the recommended budget) if the offer effect is statistically significant and the measured save rate is at least {{ rbe | pct }}; **stop** if it is clearly below; otherwise run one more wave. If the true save rate is {{ default_offer.save_rate | pct(0) }}, this rule says go {{ pilot.at_assumed.p_go | pct(0) }} of the time.
